 "subject": "Shiny App Feedback",
 "messages_listed": 7,
 "last_activity": "2017-06-01T19:03:10Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "EEGvQkEEAAAJ",
   "from": "Shawn Garbett",
   "author": "57a7662e790ccc92",
   "date": "2017-05-31T17:00:05Z",
   "subject": "Shiny App Feedback",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Our work to date is up live on <a href=\"http://rightsim.org/RIGHT\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://rightsim.org/RIGHT&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467909581000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0vy5OUCVy7cBiqQR1iIcMJ\">http://rightsim.org/RIGHT</a>. The website still has issues, but the simmer code has been working incredibly well.<div><br></div><div>One thing we would like is to have feedback on time to completion or where timewise a simulation is for user feedback. Has anyone done this with simmer and shiny? Is it possible?</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "_T_Hw7BhCgAJ",
   "from": "Mark",
   "author": "a5b521959296c79e",
   "date": "2017-05-31T18:02:50Z",
   "subject": "Re: Shiny App Feedback",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The easiest thing that comes to mind would be to just do a pilot run of a small number of replications, calculate a mean and variance and then calculate the 95th quantile as your predicted run-time.<div>I haven&#39;t looked, but I wouldn&#39;t be surprised if the progress bar in shiny allows you to provide an estimated completion time.</div><div>If you have a small number of parameters that can be changed and your analysis is static, you could also just create a surrogate model of the average/stdev runtime and use that to estimate your runtime for the selected params.  A GP/krigging model would probably be sufficient.</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "VPHymyADAQAJ",
   "from": "Iñaki Úcar",
   "author": "0f5b7adb7e70bd8b",
   "date": "2017-05-31T22:22:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: [simmer-devel] Shiny App Feedback",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Shawn,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m quite impressed, that&#39;s a great work! About the runtime, some kind of progress report is a feature that I think it&#39;s very interesting for long runs and easy to implement in simmer as an option. I&#39;ll definitely take a look at this. In the meantime, Mark&#39;s suggestions are to be considered.</div><div><br></div><div>Another simpler approach that comes to my mind, a more... heuristic approach, would be to run the model in steps. For instance, let&#39;s say that the selected simulation time is 5000. You can run(until=1000), report back to the user, run(until=2000), report back... and finally run(until=5000). Because if you increase the time, the simulation just continues from the last step. So the web interface would show: 0%, 20%, 40%... The penalty in terms of total execution time should be very small.</div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Iñaki</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2017-05-31 19:00 GMT+02:00 Shawn Garbett <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">shawn....@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span>:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Our work to date is up live on <a href=\"http://rightsim.org/RIGHT\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://rightsim.org/RIGHT&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467909583000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3ReR0-EPSIlNGmPrmBLbOF\">http://rightsim.org/RIGHT</a>. The website still has issues, but the simmer code has been working incredibly well.<div><br></div><div>One thing we would like is to have feedback on time to completion or where timewise a simulation is for user feedback. Has anyone done this with simmer and shiny? Is it possible?</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
